    "mod.what.title": "What is FBT?", "mod.what.tag": "Foundations",
    "mod.what.b1.h": "Definition",
    "mod.what.b1.p": "Forest-Based Therapy (FBT) is a structured therapeutic approach that uses natural forest environments as the primary setting for psychological and physical health interventions. It is distinct from recreational forest use — the therapeutic relationship, intentional framing, and evidence-based activities are central.",
    "mod.what.b2.h": "Distinction from Forest Bathing",
    "mod.what.b2.p": "Shinrin-yoku (forest bathing) is passive immersion. FBT is practitioner-guided, goal-oriented, and often combines somatic, creative, and relational elements within a clinical or semi-clinical framework.",
    "mod.what.b3.h": "Core mechanisms",
    "mod.what.b3.p": "Attention Restoration Theory (ART), Stress Recovery Theory (SRT), biophilia hypothesis, and the role of phytoncides and reduced cortisol load in forest environments.",
    "mod.evidence.title": "Evidence Base", "mod.evidence.tag": "Research · F4Y Findings",
    "mod.evidence.b1.h": "Mental health outcomes",
    "mod.evidence.b1.p": "FBT interventions show consistent reductions in anxiety, depression, and perceived stress across adolescent and adult populations, with moderate to strong effect sizes in controlled studies.",
    "mod.evidence.b2.h": "Young people specifically",
    "mod.evidence.b2.p": "Adolescents show particular responsiveness to outdoor therapeutic contexts — lower resistance, greater emotional openness, and stronger peer-relational effects compared to indoor settings.",
    "mod.evidence.b3.h": "Limitations to acknowledge",
    "mod.evidence.b3.p": "Heterogeneous protocols, varying session lengths, and limited RCT data make direct comparison difficult. Practitioner competency and environmental quality are strong moderators.",
    "mod.contra.title": "Contraindications", "mod.contra.tag": "Safety & Ethics",
    "mod.contra.b1.h": "Clinical contraindications",
    "mod.contra.b1.p": "Acute psychosis, severe agoraphobia or nature-related phobias, recent suicidal crisis without stabilisation, and conditions requiring close clinical supervision should be carefully assessed before outdoor referral.",
    "mod.contra.b2.h": "Environmental considerations",
    "mod.contra.b2.p": "Severe allergies (pollen, insect), extreme weather sensitivity, or significant mobility limitations require protocol adaptation, not necessarily exclusion.",
    "mod.contra.b3.h": "Ethical boundaries",
    "mod.contra.b3.p": "FBT does not replace clinical treatment. Practitioners must define their role clearly — whether they are acting as guides, facilitators, or co-therapists — and work within their competency boundaries.",
    "mod.pocket.title": "Pocket Guide to Activities",
    "detail.purpose": "Purpose", "detail.lookfor": "What to look for",
    "detail.example": "Facilitation example", "detail.suitable": "Suitable for",
    "act.sitspot.name": "Sit Spot Practice",
    "act.sitspot.purpose": "Cultivate stillness and directed sensory attention. The sit spot is a foundational FBT practice that builds capacity for present-moment awareness and reduces cognitive overload through sustained, passive observation of one natural location.",
    "act.sitspot.lookfor": "Reduced respiratory rate and visible softening of posture after 5–8 minutes. Spontaneous verbalisation or journaling about sensory detail. Resistance in first minutes is normal — particularly in adolescents.",
    "act.sitspot.example": "\"Find a place you're drawn to. Sit comfortably. For the next 15 minutes, your only task is to notice — sounds, light, movement, smell. You don't need to do anything with what you notice.\"",
    "act.map.name": "Sensory Mapping",
    "act.map.purpose": "Externalise internal states through spatial and sensory representation. Participants create a personal map of their forest space based on what they feel, not what they see — linking emotional topography to physical place.",
    "act.map.lookfor": "Emergence of emotional language through spatial metaphor. Note areas the participant avoids or is drawn to — these can become productive therapeutic material. Projective content may arise.",
    "act.map.example": "\"Walk through this area and mark on your paper the spots that feel different — safe, uncomfortable, alive, heavy. Use any symbols or words you like. You're mapping how this place feels to you, not what it looks like.\"",
    "act.walk.name": "Threshold Walk",
    "act.walk.purpose": "Use physical movement through a defined forest route as a metaphor for transition, change, or decision-making. The walk becomes a narrative container — what the participant notices mirrors what they are processing internally.",
    "act.walk.lookfor": "Pace changes, pauses, and verbal comments that link the physical environment to internal states. Spontaneous metaphor is particularly rich material. Facilitated debrief at the end of the walk is essential.",
    "act.walk.example": "\"We'll walk from here to the clearing. As we go, notice what feels hard, what feels easier, what surprises you. At the end, we'll take a few minutes to talk about what came up.\"",
    "act.council.name": "Council of Trees",
    "act.council.purpose": "A structured group reflection practice in which participants each choose a tree to identify with and speak from that position. Creates psychological distance for difficult topics while maintaining emotional engagement through embodied metaphor.",
    "act.council.lookfor": "The tree chosen and the qualities attributed to it often reveal the participant's self-concept. Listen for projections onto the tree's environment (too exposed, in the shade, isolated, intertwined with others).",
    "act.council.example": "\"Walk until you find a tree that feels like it could be you right now. Spend five minutes with it. Then we'll sit in a circle and each of us will speak as that tree — what it sees, what it needs, how it stands.\"",
    "mod.adapt.title": "Adapting Activities", "mod.adapt.tag": "For specific clinical presentations",
    "adapt.sub": "Suggested adjustments for the most common clinical pictures encountered in early sessions.",
    "mod.pre.title": "Pre-Session Checklist", "mod.pre.tag": "Preparation",
    "mod.pre.intro": "Run through this before each session. Tap each item to mark it.",
    "mod.pre.note": "This checklist does not replace a full risk assessment for clinical settings.",
    "check.pre.1": "Site assessed for safety (uneven ground, fallen branches, insects)",
    "check.pre.2": "Participant contraindications reviewed (allergies, mobility, clinical flags)",
    "check.pre.3": "Weather checked and appropriate clothing communicated",
    "check.pre.4": "Session objectives defined and activity selected",
    "check.pre.5": "Informed consent and confidentiality confirmed",
