    "mod.plan.title": "Session Structure Guide", "mod.plan.tag": "60–90 min format",
    "mod.plan.b1.h": "Opening (10–15 min)", "mod.plan.b1.p": "Arrival and grounding. Brief check-in — physical and emotional. Orient participants to the space without agenda. Let the environment land before introducing any structure.",
    "mod.plan.b2.h": "Core activity (30–45 min)", "mod.plan.b2.p": "The main intervention — one of the pocket guide activities or a practitioner-designed protocol. Hold the frame lightly: nature often redirects sessions productively.",
    "mod.plan.b3.h": "Integration (10–15 min)", "mod.plan.b3.p": "Sitting debrief in the space. Open questions: what came up, what surprised, what stays. Avoid over-interpretation — hold space rather than analyse.",
    "mod.plan.b4.h": "Transition out (5–10 min)", "mod.plan.b4.p": "Conscious re-entry. Acknowledge the shift back to everyday space. A simple closing gesture (breath, touch of bark, naming one thing) supports psychological closure.",
    "mod.reflectself.title": "Practitioner Self-Reflection", "mod.reflectself.tag": "Post-session prompts",
    "mod.reflectself.b1.h": "What did you notice in yourself?", "mod.reflectself.b1.p": "Were there moments you felt drawn to redirect, speed up, or fill silence? What does that tell you about your own relationship to the material or the environment?",
    "mod.reflectself.b2.h": "What did the environment do?", "mod.reflectself.b2.p": "Did anything in the natural space shift the session unexpectedly — weather, an animal, a sound? How did you and the participant respond? Was that response worked with or avoided?",
    "mod.reflectself.b3.h": "What would you do differently?", "mod.reflectself.b3.p": "Was the activity well-matched to the participant's state? Was the timing right? Where did the frame hold and where did it need flexibility?",
    "mod.reflectself.b4.h": "What needs to be brought to supervision?", "mod.reflectself.b4.p": "Note any countertransference, boundary moments, clinical concerns, or powerful projective material that emerged. FBT supervision should include the environmental dimension explicitly.",
    "mod.indicators.title": "Observable Outcome Indicators", "mod.indicators.tag": "What to document",
    "mod.indicators.intro": "These are non-standardised observational markers — not validated scales. Use alongside formal assessment tools.",
    "check.ind.1": "Participant appeared more physically relaxed at end than at start",
    "check.ind.2": "Spontaneous engagement with the environment (not only prompted)",
    "check.ind.3": "Use of metaphor or narrative language unprompted",
    "check.ind.4": "Expressed reluctance to leave the space",
    "check.ind.5": "Connected current session to personal context without prompting",
    "check.ind.6": "Visible emotional response (tears, laughter, stillness)",
    "mod.glossary.title": "Key Terms", "mod.glossary.tag": "Glossary",
    "mod.glossary.art": "Natural environments restore directed attention capacity by engaging involuntary fascination — the mind rests its effortful focus, reducing fatigue and improving cognitive function.",
    "mod.glossary.bio": "Wilson's hypothesis that humans have an innate affiliation with other living systems — the basis for why natural settings may uniquely support wellbeing.",
    "mod.glossary.phyto": "Volatile organic compounds released by trees, particularly conifers. Inhalation is associated with reduced cortisol, increased NK cell activity, and improved mood.",
    "mod.glossary.somatic": "In FBT, attention to body sensation as a channel for emotional information — supported by the environmental context, which naturally directs attention outward and then back inward.",
